## Why This Is Asked
Tests basic SwiftUI state management, on-device persistence, live UI updates, and accessibility considerations in a beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI @AppStorage for lightweight persistence
- Color theming and dynamic UI updates
- Accessibility labeling and dynamic type considerations
- Basic test planning: persistence, accessibility, UI consistency

## Code Example
```swift
@AppStorage("themeColor") private var themeColorHex: String = "#4285F4"
@AppStorage("fontSize") private var fontSize: Double = 14.0

var body: some View {
  VStack {
    // color palette and font size slider UI here
    Text("Preview text").font(.system(size: CGFloat(fontSize)))
      .foregroundColor(Color(hex: themeColorHex))
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate edge cases like invalid saved color values?
- How would you extend this to support dark mode vs light mode themes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests SwiftUI, Combine, and simple disk persistence in a beginner-friendly way, plus lifecycle handling.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI state management and data binding
- Combine debounce to throttle rapid changes
- Lightweight persistence to disk (Documents) with Codable or plain text
- Lifecycle considerations (background/foreground)

## Code Example
```swift
import SwiftUI
import Combine

class NoteViewModel: ObservableObject {
  @Published var text: String = ""
  @Published var wordCount: Int = 0

  private var cancellables = Set<AnyCancellable>()

  init() {
    $text
      .debounce(for: .milliseconds(300), scheduler: RunLoop.main)
      .map { $0.split(whereSeparator: { $0 == " " || $0 == "\n" }).count }
      .assign(to: \.wordCount, on: self)
      .store(in: &cancellables)
  }

  func saveToDisk() {
    // write to Documents/notes.txt
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test that debounce correctly throttles rapid input?
- How would you handle a save failure and retry strategy?

